@page "/projects/lampyr"
@layout ParkerPortfolio.Layouts.ProjectLayout

<PageTitle>Lampyr · Aaron Joel Parker</PageTitle>

<div class="canvas">
    <a class="back" href="/">&larr; Portfolio</a>

    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">
            <span class="pulse"></span>
            The @P.Archetype
        </p>
        <h1 class="title">@P.Name</h1>
        <p class="tagline">@P.Tagline</p>
        <p class="status">@P.Status</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
            A Deaf household still needs to know that the smoke alarm went off, that
            the kettle is whistling, that the baby is crying. Existing solutions either
            assault the whole house with audio or demand a control panel of fragile
            attention. Neither respects the quiet.
        </p>

        <h2>The approach</h2>
        <p>
            Lampyr listens locally with a small on-device classifier, then translates
            recognized sounds into a haptic pulse on the wrist and a soft glow on the
            wall — one pulse, the size of the moment. The kitchen smoke alarm becomes
            a wristband tap and a coral wash, and nothing else changes.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
            ONNX Runtime at the edge, Azure IoT Hub for fleet plumbing, Azure Functions
            for fan-out, MAUI for the wearable surface, and Blazor for the household
            display. Clean Architecture keeps each layer honest.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <p class="stack-label">Stack</p>
        <div class="chips">
            @foreach (var tech in P.Stack)
            {
                <span class="chip">@tech</span>
            }
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly Project P = ProjectsCatalog.All.First(p => p.Slug == "lampyr");
}
